  "statement": "Commercial aviation does not operate on unverified guesswork, despite claims that safety-critical systems rely entirely on continuous runtime patching. Federal Aviation Administration standard DO-178C requires software for airborne systems to undergo rigorous, deterministic pre-certification where every execution path is formally checked before a single passenger boards an aircraft. Equating this disciplined engineering standard with unconstrained machine learning model updates is a category error that ignores how physical safety is actually maintained.\n\nThe fantasy of autonomous software healing collapses upon inspecting the physical infrastructure required to keep foundation models operational. When frontier labs route millions of failed prediction strings to contract workers across the Global South, they are not demonstrating automated adaptation. They are operating an externalized repair shop that masks systemic model brittleness behind a facade of frictionless intelligence.\n\nThis hidden labor force absorbs the operational failures that automated benchmarks refuse to measure. While executives market real-time deployment loops as self-optimizing progress, human workers ingest toxic text and manually correct logic failures for poverty wages. The entire system relies on exploiting geographic labor arbitrage to simulate a level of reliability the software cannot achieve on its own.\n\nCapital captures the financial returns of automated deployment while the socialized costs of mental fatigue and precarious wages are dumped onto offshore data workers. Asking the public to trust live telemetry without verified pre-deployment guardrails is not innovation policy. Who absorbs the liability when uncertified code fails in high-stakes institutional settings while the vendor pockets the subscription fee?",
